1
00:00:00,759 --> 00:00:01,759
If you remember,

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:07,599
the structure we created earlier has inherited the policy from the ancestor.

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:11,380
The structure was organization, folder.

4
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:17,559
Inside the folder, we had projects, and within the project, we had resources.

5
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:20,200
Let's understand it with one example.

6
00:00:20,319 --> 00:00:24,659
Imagine your organization is the roof of a house.

7
00:00:24,879 --> 00:00:30,260
Folders are the rooms, projects are the furniture inside each room.

8
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:35,939
If it's raining outside and the roof leaks, every room gets wet.

9
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:38,520
Every piece of furniture gets wet.

10
00:00:38,799 --> 00:00:40,960
That's the IAM inheritance.

11
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:45,354
A role granted at the organizational level trickles down

12
00:00:45,354 --> 00:00:48,294
into every folder and every project.

13
00:00:48,474 --> 00:00:52,275
That's how the organizational level roles must be

14
00:00:52,275 --> 00:00:55,665
treated like high security master keys.

15
00:00:55,875 --> 00:01:02,479
For example, let's say you assign a role of compute admin to the networking team.

16
00:01:02,759 --> 00:01:05,989
Now this group can now create virtual machines,

17
00:01:06,279 --> 00:01:08,830
delete virtual machines, modify networks,

18
00:01:09,309 --> 00:01:10,629
change firewalls.

19
00:01:10,910 --> 00:01:11,739
Super powerful.

20
00:01:11,959 --> 00:01:15,959
They can do this across all the projects everywhere.

21
00:01:16,199 --> 00:01:18,870
This potentially could be super dangerous,

22
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:23,040
but absolutely necessary for the platform network teams.

23
00:01:23,279 --> 00:01:26,080
Now let's contrast this with folder‑level access.

24
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:31,260
Folders are where enterprise locks in the environment‑specific roles.

25
00:01:31,349 --> 00:01:35,180
Let's say we assign the role of viewer permission for

26
00:01:35,180 --> 00:01:38,129
the logging on a production folder.

27
00:01:38,309 --> 00:01:39,019
Now what happens?

28
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:42,349
Every project inside this production folder now

29
00:01:42,349 --> 00:01:46,069
automatically inherits this logging viewer access.

30
00:01:46,349 --> 00:01:52,150
This is fantastic for SRE teams who need to look across all the production

31
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:56,760
logs without having to request access project by project,

32
00:01:56,949 --> 00:01:59,940
this approach gives you the consistency,

33
00:02:00,059 --> 00:02:03,519
easy onboarding, and clear safety boundaries.

34
00:02:03,699 --> 00:02:07,339
It also prevents the classic mistake of giving

35
00:02:07,339 --> 00:02:09,800
developers the production write access.

36
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:12,520
If you talk about the project‑level roles,

37
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:13,380
for example,

38
00:02:13,529 --> 00:02:17,169
let's imagine the payment engineering team needs

39
00:02:17,169 --> 00:02:20,600
read‑only access to view the metric and log,

40
00:02:21,059 --> 00:02:22,380
and we provide them.

41
00:02:22,970 --> 00:02:26,229
This is specific permission and nothing more.

42
00:02:26,449 --> 00:02:28,210
They should not deploy infrastructure.

43
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:30,009
They should not modify IAM.

44
00:02:30,369 --> 00:02:32,429
They should not have a billing permission.

45
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:35,740
This is least privilege in action.

46
00:02:35,970 --> 00:02:38,470
Let's compare two real‑world scenarios.

47
00:02:38,610 --> 00:02:41,089
A bad pattern, just give them the editor access.

48
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:42,560
It's easier.

49
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:47,160
If you give someone editor, they can delete your VPC,

50
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:52,899
modify IAM, turn off logging, and maybe they can stop the production jobs.

51
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:55,460
Editor is simply too powerful.

52
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:59,610
These roles represent thousands, yes, thousands of the permissions,

53
00:02:59,610 --> 00:03:01,869
and that is why it is not good.

54
00:03:02,009 --> 00:03:06,970
The good pattern would be is give them exactly the predefined roles they need.

55
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:12,369
For example, let's say a developer needs to deploy cloud‑run services,

56
00:03:12,729 --> 00:03:15,370
view logs, and read configuration.

57
00:03:15,370 --> 00:03:19,479
Then you should define the exact role for that.

58
00:03:19,649 --> 00:03:26,119
These predefined roles contain exactly the permission needed and nothing extra.

59
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:31,729
The correct roles would be run.developer, logging.viewer, and monitoring.viewer.

60
00:03:31,929 --> 00:03:36,195
These predefined roles contain exactly the permission needed.

61
00:03:36,345 --> 00:03:37,716
Let's take another example.

62
00:03:37,916 --> 00:03:42,195
Another bad pattern would be a giant custom role with 200

63
00:03:42,195 --> 00:03:45,026
permissions becomes impossible to audit.

64
00:03:45,186 --> 00:03:48,865
The good pattern would be the small, purpose‑built custom role,

65
00:03:49,095 --> 00:03:52,315
and only when it is necessary, for example,

66
00:03:52,395 --> 00:03:58,052
a role containing exactly the 12 permissions needed for a pipeline to run tests.

67
00:03:59,061 --> 00:04:02,632
Another example would be giving an IAM to individuals.

68
00:04:02,781 --> 00:04:04,871
This becomes a maintenance nightmare.

69
00:04:05,102 --> 00:04:08,302
The good pattern would be grant IAM to the groups.

70
00:04:08,501 --> 00:04:11,322
Team evolves, but permission should not, right?

71
00:04:11,582 --> 00:04:12,141
In fact,

72
00:04:12,401 --> 00:04:15,781
the Google's recommendation is managing the permission using

73
00:04:15,781 --> 00:04:18,940
groups to simplify the access control.
